  "readme": [
    {
      "section": "dsh-project-memory",
      "text": "Evidence-backed, human-approved project memory for [DeepSeek Harness](https://github.com/deepseek-ai/deepseek-harness).\n\n`dsh-project-memory` turns durable decisions, constraints, conventions, lessons, and facts into versioned project records. The model may propose a candidate, but only the human-facing `/memory approve` command can promote it into approved memory."
    },
    {
      "section": "dsh-project-memory",
      "text": "> DeepSeek Harness is currently a developer preview. This plugin targets `0.1.0-rc.6` and may require updates as Harness APIs evolve.\n\n[中文说明](./README.zh.md)"
    },
    {
      "section": "Why this is different",
      "text": "- Human approval is a hard boundary: model tools cannot approve or archive memory.\n- Evidence is project-relative and hash-verified; source snippets are not copied into memory.\n- Conflicting active memories require `supersedes` or an explicit `--keep-both` decision.\n- Approved entries are Git-friendly JSON; candidate files are kept in a separate review queue.\n- Search is deterministic and local. No embeddings, cloud database, or background service."
    },
    {
      "section": "Install",
      "text": "```bash\ndsh plugin --profile web add dsh-project-memory\n```\n\nRestart the selected Harness profile after installation."
    },
    {
      "section": "Commands",
      "text": "```text\n/memory capture [note]\n/memory candidates\n/memory approve <candidate-id> [--keep-both]\n/memory reject <candidate-id> [reason]\n\nThe plugin also exposes read-only model tools for search/read/validation and one write-limited proposal tool."
    },
    {
      "section": "Storage",
      "text": "```text\n.dsh/project.json\n.dsh/memory/index.json\n.dsh/memory/entries/memory_*.json\n.dsh/memory/candidates/candidate_*.json\n\nCommit `.dsh/project.json`, `.dsh/memory/index.json`, and `.dsh/memory/entries/` if the team wants shared memory. Candidate files may remain local until reviewed."
    },
    {
      "section": "Evidence lifecycle",
      "text": "Each evidence reference records a relative path, exact line range, optional symbol, and SHA-256 hash. `/memory validate` reports `verified`, `stale`, or `missing` after source changes."
    },
    {
      "section": "Development",
      "text": "```bash\nnpm install\nnpm run check\nnpm pack --dry-run\n```\n\nMIT © 2026"
    }
  ],
